 \subsection{The exact structure of the controlled map}
 \label{ctrl:structure}
+
+The distinction between the original and controlled structures is explicit in the general construction. Let $G(z,t)\in\R^{2n\times m}$ be the actual input matrix and let $a_u(z,t)\in\R^m$ be a prescribed smooth feedback. On a simply connected local domain the same $\mathcal K$ is compatible with $f_u=f+Ga_u$ precisely when the one-form with coefficients $\mathcal KGa_u$ is closed; equivalently, a scalar $W$ satisfies
+\begin{equation}\label{ctrl:same-K}
+\begin{aligned}
+ \mathcal KGa_u&=\nabla W,\\
+ \mathcal Kf_u&=\nabla(\mathcal B+W)+\partial_t\mathcal R.
+\end{aligned}
+\end{equation}
+The condition also requires $\nabla W\in\operatorname{Im}(\mathcal KG)$. Small input magnitude alone is insufficient. If it fails, applying \eqref{struct:transport} to the actual $f_u$ gives a new local $\mathcal R_u,\mathcal K_u,\mathcal B_u$ and its own compatible chart. The resulting ideal map preserves $\mathcal K_u$, not necessarily the original $\mathcal K$; Supplement~\ref{ss:general-control} gives the argument.
+
+For the present plant, with initial time $\tau_0=0$, $\mathcal K_\gamma=-e^{\gamma\tau}J$. Pure potential compensation satisfies $\mathcal K_\gamma(0,\nabla\widehat R)^{\mathsf T}=\nabla_x[-e^{\gamma\tau}\widehat R]$, so it can retain the same structure. The damping compensation term $(0,\widehat\gamma Y)^{\mathsf T}$ generally fails the closedness condition. After both terms are substituted, the explicit closed-loop structure is $\mathcal K_{\delta\gamma}=-e^{\delta\gamma\tau}J$, with the same initial value $-J$. This connects the general construction directly to \eqref{ctrl:weighted-midpoint} and to the conservative limit below.
 
